<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  com.claude.compressor-sentinel — the guard for the axis that has killed this box three times in six
  days (docs/research/panic-compressor-2026-08-05.md). VM-compressor SEGMENT exhaustion: the kernel
  reads `"compressor_exhausted": 1` while 20 GB is free, swap is idle and pressure is normal, so every
  level-based sensor on this machine reports a healthy box at the moment of death.

  THERE IS NO ProcessType KEY, AND ITS ABSENCE IS THE MOST LOAD-BEARING LINE IN THIS FILE.
  It must be neither Background nor Adaptive, and this is measured, not theorised:
    · Background: com.claude.capacity-alarm ran at ProcessType Background through the 2026-07-30
      panic-reboot and then emitted NOTHING FOR 6 HOURS 24 MINUTES — a 23,749 s inter-sample gap
      against a configured 600 s — because the box came up under a Spotlight reindex at load 140 and
      Darwin defers the Background band aggressively under exactly that load. A sensor that stops
      sampling when the machine is busy is inert precisely when it is needed.
    · Adaptive: capacity-alarm's fix, and correct FOR CAPACITY REPORTING. It is not enough here.
      §6 discriminator 4 of the research is that the background-band sampler's own DEATH — the
      absence of its row — was the earliest machine-readable distress signal in all three events,
      firing ~3 minutes before the wedge. That makes "which band does the sentinel live in" a
      correctness question, not a politeness one: the guard for an event must not be in a band the
      event is known to starve.
    · Omitting the key entirely leaves the job in the DEFAULT (Standard) band — the same treatment an
      interactive process gets. That is the point. Nice and LowPriorityIO are omitted for the same
      reason: every one of those keys is a request to be deprioritised, and this is the one job on
      the box that must not be.

  WHY THAT IS AFFORDABLE. Measured on this 10-core box, 5 trials each, INCLUDING bash startup (which
  the daemon pays once, not per tick — so these are upper bounds):
        no-census tick   0.05 s
        census tick      0.09 s   (one extra `ps -axwwo`, every 6th tick)
  At the 10 s cadence that is (5 x 0.05 + 0.09) / 60 = 0.57% of ONE core, i.e. ~0.06% of the box.
  Nothing in the tick shells out to anything expensive, and zprint — the only instrument that can
  read the descriptor count directly — is BANNED from the tick, because it hangs under the very storm
  it measures (the 00:14 run was still TH_WAIT at panic). §7.7's cheap-sysctl recipe replaces it.

  KeepAlive true / RunAtLoad true / NO StartInterval — because the CADENCE LIVES IN THE SCRIPT.
  This is an internal-loop daemon, not a periodic job: launchd's entire responsibility here is to
  start it at load and restart it if it ever dies. A StartInterval job re-pays process startup on
  every sample and, more importantly, cannot hold the previous sample — and every trip term in this
  sentinel is a RATE, which needs a baseline that survives between reads. KeepAlive is what makes the
  guard's own death self-healing; RunAtLoad is what makes it present after the panic reboot, which is
  the exact moment the next burst tends to arrive.

  THE PATH MUST CARRY /usr/sbin, AND THIS IS NOT A STYLE DETAIL. launchd hands a job
  PATH=/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin, but the wrapper below OVERWRITES it. `sysctl` lives in
  /usr/sbin; `vm_stat` in /usr/bin; `ps`, `date`, `sleep` in /bin. capacity-alarm.sh shipped this
  wrapper with /usr/sbin omitted and silently lost THREE rungs in every scheduled run (never in a
  hand-run from an interactive shell, which is why it survived review). Here the consequence would be
  different and louder rather than silent — an unreadable sysctl SKIPS the tick and logs to stderr
  rather than rendering as 0 — but the sentinel would still be totally blind.

  THE ACTUATOR WAS ARMED 2026-08-09 04:3x PDT — operator authorization "arm it" in-session, after
  watchdog panics #5 (03:39) and #6 (04:17) the same night. CC_SENTINEL_ACT=stop is exported in the
  wrapper below: on a trip the sentinel SIGSTOPs the newest ^node-comm burst cohort (RSS ≥100 MB,
  cap 200) — never claude/mcp-shaped processes, never SIGKILL; SIGSTOP is reversible (SIGCONT).
  Disarm: delete the export from the wrapper (or CC_SENTINEL=off, or launchctl bootout
  gui/$UID/com.claude.compressor-sentinel).
  Cohort tuning, same authorization: ACT_RSS_KB=40960 (40 MB — the 08-09 04:08/04:13 storm's 372-736
  node procs averaged 62-75 MB, UNDER the default 100 MB floor, so the stock floor would have spared
  most of the horde) and ACT_CAP=400 (waves measured at 372-736 members; the default 200/trip covers
  barely half a wave between 60 s cooldowns).
  (Pre-arming rationale, kept for history: arming is an operator decision, made by adding
  CC_SENTINEL_ACT=stop to the wrapper — deliberately not a maintenance edit, because it is the
  first thing on this box that acts on live processes without being asked. Detection-only mode
  wrote pages that nothing could act on in the 5-minute storm window; detection alone saved
  nothing in four consecutive panics. Full evidence: docs/research/crash-rootcause-2026-08-09.md.)

  Kill switches after load: CC_SENTINEL=off in the wrapper, or
  `launchctl bootout gui/$UID/com.claude.compressor-sentinel`.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.claude.compressor-sentinel</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>-c</string>
        <string>export PATH="$HOME/.claude/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin"; export CC_SENTINEL_ACT=stop; export CC_SENTINEL_ACT_RSS_KB=40960; export CC_SENTINEL_ACT_CAP=400; exec "$HOME/.claude/scripts/compressor-sentinel.sh"</string>
    </array>

    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>

    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- NO ProcessType. NO Nice. NO LowPriorityIO. See the header — the background band starves
         first, and this is the job that must survive the storm it exists to report. -->

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>  <string>/tmp/claude-compressor-sentinel.stdout.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>/tmp/claude-compressor-sentinel.stderr.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
